			<div class="content" id="post-content-2300522">
									

<p>Sure, and that's why I asked the author about this. I was actually surprised by the answer myself.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>That said, my real point is that the D-Class conducting the interview seems very unlikely to me. She's a 29 year old woman, seems to be trusted by the research staff enough to to this and seems to be a native English speaker.</p>
<ul>
<li>Only 2% of death row inmates are female.</li>
<li>Only 48.2% have even a High School education.</li>
<li>10% are seriously mentally ill.</li>
<li>All of them are convicted of serious murders and in most states must either be mass murderers or have &quot;special circumstances&quot; that make the crime especially heinous (like prolonged torture).</li>
</ul>
<p>Or she's one of those refugee or political prisoner ones, I guess. In that case, I'm not sure what's motivating her to do this (and I also personally think this &quot;other&quot; D-Class should be considered a lot less disposable from an ethical stand point).</p>
<p>I guess I don't think it makes sense to send an unreliable and disposable person to do this job, but rather somebody with some training and loyalty. I also think I don't like using the slave labor testing force, except when you really must.</p>
</div>
</div>
